title Caravan queue -- a ring says something happened, and the queue says how much
prompt Autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Microkernel Target double-seat, Standfast on reds first, one council-rota doc deep-read, condense REMEMBER, send the finished increment.
think Standfast first. Read crux/REDS.md whole -- nine rows, every one carrying a closure note (#81 #82 #83 #84 #85 twice #86 #87), the newest closed on measurement last lap. No open agent-closable red stands, so the constructive queue opens honestly.
think Council rota, lap 455 of the 27-doc d27 -- item 23, context/specs/20260627-102012_one-clock-naming-law.md. Its lesson landed on this round twice: the module's own version string and this log's filename both come from one live read of TZ=America/New_York date, never from a stamp remembered off the last lap. Later is always larger, and nothing is renumbered.
think The running thread from session-logs/README.md names the crux plainly -- unprompted.rye closed on "the many-ask shape: more than one ask outstanding on one channel at a time, where a single counted bell stops being enough to say how much happened." Fifteen rings stood; this is the sixteenth.
think Read the whole idiom before writing any of it -- unprompted.rye end to end, serve.rye's message helpers, exercise.rye's Reach with its two walls, roundtrip.rye's directed bells, and the serial_two_clients declaration. The arc's motif is that a new property needs no new wiring, and I wanted to know whether that held here before promising it.
think The crux stated properly: a doorbell coalesces. Rings arriving before the receiver runs are indistinguishable from one, and a producer batching many asks behind a single ring is doing the efficient thing rather than a wrong one. So the ring count never answers how many asks are waiting -- only shared memory does, with two indices.
think Then the finding I did not expect going in. The two indices cannot share a region, because each must live in memory its own writer holds. A producer's head belongs in the region the producer writes; a consumer's tail in the region the consumer writes. The declaration already grants exactly that pair per channel, so the queue spans BOTH regions -- and that is not a compromise, it is the property: a producer able to advance the tail could claim its asks were consumed, and a consumer able to advance the head could invent asks nobody made. Ownership does the work a lock would otherwise pretend to do.
think Order carries one level in from notify.rye. There the bytes were written before the bell, since a bell rung early sends a reader to look at nothing. Here the slots are written before the head, since a head raised early promises a consumer messages that have not landed -- and the head, rather than the bell, is what a drain believes. Same lesson, one layer down, and it lives in the code rather than in a comment.
think Capacity had to refuse rather than wrap. A ring buffer that silently overwrites an unread ask turns a bounded queue into a lossy one, so the producer reads the tail, computes what stands outstanding, and answers QueueFull -- a refusal an operator can see instead of a message that quietly went missing. The paired report is nothing_new: a ring arrived, the queue was read, and zero is the honest answer.
think GREEN on the first metal pass, so both RED paths were proven before the green was trusted. Driving the drain from the bell count rather than from head - tail carried one ask of three, and the producer caught it by reading the consumer's own tail standing at 1 against a head of 3 -- which is exactly why the settled verb exists. Dropping the capacity refusal let a fifth ask land in a four-slot queue, and flipping that step's expectation showed the real harm: the next drain read a sequence number it never expected and answered bytes differed. The overwrite was not hypothetical; it was watched.
obs The witness measures the claim in numbers rather than describing it -- 7 asks carried on 3 rings across 3 drains, with 3 of them landing behind a single ring. A consumer draining once per ring would print a different pair, so the assertion cannot pass by accident.
obs The parseInt ratchet turned on touch, 27 sites down to 25. A plan word's count now parses strictly, so "03" is refused rather than read as 3 -- and that refusal earned its own check in the wire test beside the unknown-verb one.
obs One bound stayed deliberately small: max_outstanding is four, so a batch larger than the queue is reachable inside a witness and the refusal is proven rather than asserted in prose.
obs The next crux surfaced while writing this one -- two producers on one queue, where a single head stops being enough to say who wrote which ask. Booked in REMEMBER rather than started here; one keystone per round.
loom witness=caravan_queue_witness result=GREEN asks=7 rings=3 drains=3 slots=4 bells=4 steps=13
file caravan/queue.rye the sixteenth ring -- two owned indices, a drain bounded by the queue, QueueFull and nothing_new named
file tools/caravan_queue_witness.rish proves the claim from outside the program four ways, natively read, both RED paths recorded
file caravan/README.md the ladder row and the prose section on why the queue spans two regions -- synced in the same commit
file crux/REMEMBER.md live edge moved to the queue ring, prior edge condensed into the arc pointer, git nib refreshed
recommend keep-going -- next the many-producer shape: two clients writing one queue, where a single head stops being enough to say who wrote which ask. No key, no funds.
